{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-17126" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $2,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-17126" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=2,\\,\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\ x & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\ y & =2(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-17126" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ec670a452a22e838ebb6b229321fd86f8e8dab9880e01ebbdfef439a8167b7ce"} {"question" "q-17126" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-17126" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})"] ["math" "(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{3})"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-17126" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-17128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $2,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-17128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=2,\\,\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2(-1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(0,-2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-17128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ec670a452a22e838ebb6b229321fd86f8e8dab9880e01ebbdfef439a8167b7ce"} {"question" "q-17128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-17128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-2,0)"] ["math" "(0,-2)"] ["math" "(0,1)"] ["math" "(0,2)"] ["math" "(2,0)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-17128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(0,-2)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-40804" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $({r}^{2},\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-40804" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n((\\sqrt{10})^{2},\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=(10,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =10(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =10(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-5,-5\\sqrt{3}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-40804" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ec670a452a22e838ebb6b229321fd86f8e8dab9880e01ebbdfef439a8167b7ce"} {"question" "q-40804" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-40804" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-5,-5\\sqrt{3})"] ["math" "(-5\\sqrt{3},-5)"] ["math" "(-\\sqrt{5},-3\\sqrt{5})"] ["math" "(-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{15})"] ["math" "(-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-40804" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-5,-5\\sqrt{3})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-120598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/assets/475bda1ee758774ec9b07ec12a0ed92e56c4b571fd39f6e1ecca439278106ea6.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(2,{240}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={195}^{∘},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-120598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(2,{240}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=2.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $2,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $2.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${240}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={240}^{∘}-{195}^{∘}={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(2,{45}^{∘})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-120598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ec670a452a22e838ebb6b229321fd86f8e8dab9880e01ebbdfef439a8167b7ce"} {"question" "q-120598" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-120598" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(1,1)"] ["math" "(2,2)"] ["math" "(2,\\sqrt{2})"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2},2)"] ["math" "(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-120598" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-276807" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$ Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(2\\sqrt{3},2).$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-276807" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot 4,\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=(8,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-4\\sqrt{3},-4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-276807" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ec670a452a22e838ebb6b229321fd86f8e8dab9880e01ebbdfef439a8167b7ce"} {"question" "q-276807" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-276807" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13968929/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba02fe65dad82c8829c85dd5d19d595fb67db6f574bef2a490e4bca5c5c2939d"} {"question" "q-276807" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Aca